            <ArticleTitle>Serum homocysteine levels in acne patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/jocd.12456</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study was designed to investigate serum homocysteine (HCY) levels in acne patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Acne patients (n = 124) and healthy volunteers (n = 70), matched in terms of both age and sex, were enrolled. Serum HCY levels for all subjects were measured by a clinical laboratory.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Serum HCY levels in male and female patients with severe and moderate acne were significantly higher than in the healthy control group (P &lt; .05). The constituent ratio of male and female acne patients with HCY above the normal range (10 mmol/L) was significantly higher than the healthy control group. The severity of acne patients was positively correlated with serum homocysteine concentration, (P &lt; .01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Hyperhomocysteinemia may be an independent risk factor for acne vulgaris. Detection of serum HCY is important for acne patients.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2017 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
